<EXPERIMENT_PACKAGE><EXPERIMENT accession="SRX24283134" alias="Rep2 daf2 reads"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRX24283134</PRIMARY_ID></IDENTIFIERS><TITLE>single nucleus RNA-seq of Adult C. elegans neurons</TITLE><STUDY_REF accession="SRP502277"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRP502277</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioProject">PRJNA1027859</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS></STUDY_REF><DESIGN><DESIGN_DESCRIPTION>Single nuclei suspension samples were loaded to the 10X Genomics Chromium X system using the Single Cell 3 v3.1 Reagent Kits (10X Genomics Inc., CA) to generate and amplify cDNA. Up to 30,000 nuclei were loaded per 10X sample. The amplified cDNA samples were purified with Ampure XP magnetic beads (Beckman Coulter, CA), quantified by Qubit fluorometer (Invitrogen, CA), and examined on Bioanalyzer with High Sensitivity DNA chips (Agilent, CA) for size distribution. Illumina sequencing libraries were generated from the amplified cDNA samples using the Illumina Tagment DNA Enzyme and Buffer kit (Illumina, CA). These libraries were examined by Qubit and Bioanalyzer, then pooled at equal molar amount and sequenced on Illumina NovaSeq 6000 S Prime flowcells as 28+94 nt pair-end reads following the standard protocol.  Raw sequencing reads were filtered by Illumina NovaSeq Control Software and only the Pass-Filter (PF) reads were used for further analysis.</DESIGN_DESCRIPTION><SAMPLE_DESCRIPTOR accession="SRS21047784"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS21047784</PRIMARY_ID></IDENTIFIERS></SAMPLE_DESCRIPTOR><LIBRARY_DESCRIPTOR><LIBRARY_NAME>Rep2 daf2 reads</LIBRARY_NAME><LIBRARY_STRATEGY>RNA-Seq</LIBRARY_STRATEGY><LIBRARY_SOURCE>TRANSCRIPTOMIC SINGLE CELL</LIBRARY_SOURCE><LIBRARY_SELECTION>cDNA</LIBRARY_SELECTION><LIBRARY_LAYOUT><PAIRED/></LIBRARY_LAYOUT></LIBRARY_DESCRIPTOR></DESIGN><PLATFORM><ILLUMINA><INSTRUMENT_MODEL>Illumina NovaSeq 6000</INSTRUMENT_MODEL></ILLUMINA></PLATFORM></EXPERIMENT><SUBMISSION lab_name="Molecular Biology" center_name="Princeton University" accession="SRA1847283" alias="SUB14370685"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRA1847283</PRIMARY_ID><SUBMITTER_ID namespace="Princeton University">SUB14370685</SUBMITTER_ID></IDENTIFIERS></SUBMISSION><Organization type="institute"><Name>Princeton University</Name><Address postal_code="08540"><Department>Molecular Biology</Department><Institution>Princeton University</Institution><Street>160 Washington Rd</Street><City>Princeton</City><Sub>New Jersey</Sub><Country>USA</Country></Address><Contact email="jange@princeton.edu" sec_email="jonathan.st.ange2@gmail.com"><Address postal_code="08540"><Department>Molecular Biology</Department><Institution>Princeton University</Institution><Street>160 Washington Rd</Street><City>Princeton</City><Sub>New Jersey</Sub><Country>USA</Country></Address><Name><First>Jonathan</First><Last>St. Ange</Last></Name></Contact></Organization><STUDY center_name="BioProject" alias="PRJNA1027859" accession="SRP502277"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRP502277</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioProject" label="primary">PRJNA1027859</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS><DESCRIPTOR><STUDY_TITLE>A Single-Nucleus Atlas of Adult C. elegans Neurons Reveals GPCR and Insulin-signaling Profiles</STUDY_TITLE><STUDY_TYPE existing_study_type="Other"/><STUDY_ABSTRACT>The invariant lineage of each of C. elegans 302 neurons is known, and most adult neurons have specific functions. Previously, we determined the transcriptome of C. elegans major tissues, including neurons. However, the transcripts that allow specific functions for each individual adult neuron, including each neurons complement of chemoreceptors, are unknown. The insulin-insulin-like growth factor signaling (IIS) pathway regulates many aspects of an organisms biological functions, including lifespan and memory ability. While whole-worm and tissue-specific transcriptomic analyses have identified IIS targets, a higher resolution single-cell approach is required to answer questions about individual neuronal changes that confer neuron-specific improvements. We used the deep neuronal resolution of single-nucleus RNA-sequencing to identify and characterize GPCRs that have specific functions in wild-type and daf-2 mutants in specific neurons.</STUDY_ABSTRACT><CENTER_PROJECT_NAME>Caenorhabditis elegans</CENTER_PROJECT_NAME></DESCRIPTOR></STUDY><SAMPLE alias="single nucleus sequencing daf2 replicate 2" accession="SRS21047784"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS21047784</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioSample">SAMN40935620</EXTERNAL_ID><SUBMITTER_ID namespace="pda|jange@princeton.edu" label="Sample name">single nucleus sequencing daf2 replicate 2</SUBMITTER_ID></IDENTIFIERS><SAMPLE_NAME><TAXON_ID>6239</TAXON_ID><SCIENTIFIC_NAME>Caenorhabditis elegans</SCIENTIFIC_NAME></SAMPLE_NAME><SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_LINK><XREF_LINK><DB>bioproject</DB><ID>1027859</ID><LABEL>PRJNA1027859</LABEL></XREF_LINK></SAMPLE_LINK></SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTES><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>strain</TAG><VALUE>CQ758</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>dev_stage</TAG><VALUE>Adult Day 1</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>collection_date</TAG><VALUE>2023-03-23</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>geo_loc_name</TAG><VALUE>not applicable</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>sex</TAG><VALUE>hermaphrodite</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>tissue</TAG><VALUE>Neurons</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>BioSampleModel</TAG><VALUE>Model organism or animal</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTES></SAMPLE><Pool><Member member_name="" accession="SRS21047784" sample_name="single nucleus sequencing daf2 replicate 2" spots="519832370" bases="63419549140" tax_id="6239" organism="Caenorhabditis elegans"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS21047784</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioSample">SAMN40935620</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS></Member></Pool><RUN_SET runs="1" bases="63419549140" spots="519832370" bytes="19903260404"><RUN accession="SRR28716554" alias="2495__daf-2_R2_library-r